   "i": 1,
   "number": 1,
   "section": 1,
   "type": "QCS",
   "stem": "Which of the following statements is correct regarding insulin glargine?",
   "options": [
    {
     "letter": "A",
     "text": "It is primarily used to control postprandial hyperglycemia.",
     "bold": 0.0,
     "color": 0.0,
     "marks": [],
     "page": 0,
     "bbox": [
      72.0,
      142.87623596191406,
      319.8984375,
      152.87623596191406
     ]
    },
    {
     "letter": "B",
     "text": "It is a \"peakless\" insulin.",
     "bold": 0.0,
     "color": 0.0,
     "marks": [],
     "page": 0,
     "bbox": [
      72.0,
      154.37623596191406,
      185.8203125,
      164.37623596191406
     ]
    },
    {
     "letter": "C",
     "text": "The prolonged duration of activity is due to slow dissociation from albumin.",
     "bold": 0.0,
     "color": 0.0,
     "marks": [],
     "page": 0,
     "bbox": [
      72.0,
      165.87623596191406,
      391.546875,
      175.87623596191406
     ]
    },
    {
     "letter": "D",
     "text": "It should not be used in a regimen with insulin lispro or glulisine.",
     "bold": 0.0,
     "color": 0.0,
     "marks": [],
     "page": 0,
     "bbox": [
      72.0,
      177.37362670898438,
      348.5390625,
      187.37623596191406
     ]
    }
   ],
   "after": "",
   "exp": "Insulin glargine has a relatively flat, prolonged hypoglycemic effect. Because of this, it is used for basal glucose control, not postprandial. The prolonged duration is due to its low pH, which leads to precipitation at the injection site and resultant extended action. Insulin glargine is often used for basal control in a regimen where insulin lispro, glulisine, or aspart are used for mealtime glucose control. [Note: Glargine should not be combined with other insulins in the same syringe, as it may alter the pharmacodynamic properties of the medication.]",
   "inline_answer": "B",
   "flags": [],
   "cut_prefix": "",
   "page": 0,
